\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNPCNdeletion1
\item[* relevant examples:] many, some, both, those etc.
\item[* language:] English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] NPformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)
\item[* date of creation:] 16-FEB-1989 13:13:34  
\item[* author:] Franciska deJong
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Deletion of empty count CN.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}

\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
\begin{enumerate} 
\item In Dutch there are rules for CN-deletion that probably should be replaced
by an ellipsis treatment. E.g.  In de tuin staan er veel (rozen) --> In the
garden there are many roses. NB. not: ones 

\item Dutch numerals that may occur with quantificational {\em er} can only be 
translated into a bare English numeral if they refer to human entities. As
this information is not available in generation we cannot handle these cases
properly in syntax.
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
